lesson physics-how-far-how-long v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=5 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "How far, how long"
  goal "Add the legs of a journey, work out a missing leg, and compare two ways of getting somewhere."

  look {
    scene 33x22 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        row top space=2 align=start {
          roaddistances road stops=["Ash", "Bray", "Cole"] legs=[12, 18] unit=km
          stopwatch watch seconds=25
        }
        doubleline pace steps=5 topTo=50 bottomTo=5 topUnit="km" bottomUnit=hours mark=3
      }
    }
    say """
      A journey is made of legs, and the legs add up. If you know the whole journey and one leg,
      the other leg is what is left. The bottom line on the last picture counts hours and the top
      line counts kilometres, so the two lines together say how far you get in a given time.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.journey-total count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.journey-back count=2 seed=3
    practice physics.journey-leg count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.journey-compare count=2 seed=4
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.journey-shorter a=8 b=9 c=11 easy-a=6 easy-b=6 easy-c=8 hard-a=10 hard-b=10 hard-c=12
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.journey-three a=7 b=10 c=6 easy-a=5 easy-b=6 easy-c=4 hard-a=9 hard-b=12 hard-c=8
  }
  remember {
    say "The legs of a journey add up. Take the legs you know from the whole to find the one you do not."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Use a real trip the family makes. Read the distances off a map or a signpost, add them, then
    ask about the trip home, which is the part children forget to double. The missing leg questions
    run the same addition backwards, which is harder than it looks at seven.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the legs are six to eight kilometres and the two ways to Cole differ by four kilometres or more."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the legs are twelve to fourteen kilometres, the two ways to Cole differ by one kilometre, and the three-leg road is nearly thirty kilometres long."
  }
}
